# Device identity & security

The control plane is a path into a device that may sit next to machine control.
A compromised broker or topic must not be enough to force an unverified model
onto the device.

## Non-negotiable baseline

1. **Per-device identity** — X.509 client certificates (secure element / TPM
   when available on the device). Degraded storage of keys without SE/TPM is
   an ADR with expiry, not silent default.
2. **mTLS** to the broker — no anonymous or password-only MQTT in production
   without an ADR with expiry.
3. **Per-device topic ACLs** — a device cannot impersonate another.
4. **Independent on-device signature verification** before activation — the
   agent can refuse an artifact even if the cloud "approved" it (last line of
   defence).
5. **Provisioning preconditions** (set at provisioning, not learned over the
   network): device identity (cert/key), broker endpoint, and trust anchors
   (e.g. Uptane / TUF root). Without these pre-planted, there is a
   trust-on-first-use hole.

## Secrets and logging

- Never log private keys, cert PEMs, or tokens — even partially, even at debug.
- Secret **values** are not constitution content; rotation and vault mechanics
  live in consumer ops / IAC.

